{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_01cdc78e75f6559b0169bb444190508191ab97cd379c901c4c", "created": 1773880385, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "{\n  \"title\": \"Test Results, Treatment Planning, and Patient-Directed Cancer Care\",\n  \"summary\": \"This community centers on how TEST RESULTS feed into DIAGNOSIS and subsequent TREATMENT PLAN decisions, with multiple supporting components that shape or operationalize care. Key decision inputs include RISK LEVEL (used to determine whether treatment is needed and what plan to choose), core diagnostic modalities (IMAGING STUDIES, BLOOD AND TISSUE TESTS, and BIOPSIES), and practical constraints such as AVAILABILITY AND AFFORDABILITY of DRUGS. The community also includes patient-facing information workflows—ONLINE PORTALS/PORTAL access, instructions to obtain and keep COPIES OF TEST RESULTS and cancer-type reports, and guidance that results may arrive over DAYS OR WEEKS—alongside explicit emphasis that PATIENT PREFERENCES (WISHES AND PREFERENCES) should always be integrated into treatment planning.\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"TEST RESULTS are the hub linking diagnostic confirmation to downstream care decisions\",\n      \"explanation\": \"TEST RESULTS are described as aggregated outputs from medical evaluations used to confirm or refine a diagnosis and characterize disease in ways that shape staging, risk assessment, and treatment planning [Data: Entities (154); Relationships (219)].\\n\\nThe community explicitly links TEST RESULTS to DIAGNOSIS: diagnosis is stated to be determined by a series of test results, making the test-result node the central dependency for clinical determination of disease [Data: Relationships (219); Entities (154)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"The TREATMENT PLAN is explicitly multi-factor: clinical evidence plus patient preferences and real-world constraints\",\n      \"explanation\": \"A TREATMENT PLAN is defined as an individualized, documented course of action chosen after reviewing diagnostic and staging information (including imaging, pathology/histology, and biomarker/molecular/genetic findings) and is updated as circumstances and results evolve [Data: Entities (198); Entities (85)].\\n\\nPatient direction is not optional in this network: PATIENT PREFERENCES (and the closely aligned WISHES AND PREFERENCES concept) are stated to be “always important” and should be consistently taken into account when selecting the TREATMENT PLAN [Data: Relationships (2428, 13351); Entities (1493, 5896, 198)].\\n\\nTreatment selection is also constrained by practicality: AVAILABILITY AND AFFORDABILITY are stated as factors used when choosing bladder cancer treatment, and the dataset ties this specifically to DRUGS as the constrained resource [Data: Relationships (13352, 13406); Entities (5895, 1626, 198)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"RISK LEVEL functions as a decision pivot from assessment to action\",\n      \"explanation\": \"RISK LEVEL is defined as an assessed or categorized degree of cancer risk (including examples in lung and prostate cancer contexts), used to judge aggressiveness and whether treatment is needed [Data: Entities (1062)].\\n\\nThe community encodes a direct pathway from RISK LEVEL to TREATMENT PLAN: once risk level is identified, the team decides whether treatment is needed and what the treatment plan will be, positioning risk stratification as a trigger for therapy selection and planning [Data: Relationships (6606); Entities (1062, 198)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Diagnostic modalities (imaging, biopsies, and blood/tissue testing) are framed as key inputs that produce actionable results\",\n      \"explanation\": \"IMAGING STUDIES are described as scan-based evaluations used to assess tumor size/location and spread, and their results are incorporated with other clinical information to inform clinical decision-making and determine an appropriate treatment plan [Data: Entities (85)].\\n\\nThe relationships further specify that results from BLOOD AND TISSUE TESTS help determine the treatment plan, and that BIOPSIES produce test results (often summarized in pathology reports) used to determine the treatment plan—reinforcing that multiple testing streams converge into the planning decision [Data: Relationships (12864, 13234); Entities (5727, 5817, 154, 198)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Radiation therapy planning is operationalized via plan parameters (dose and number of sessions) and modality selection (EBRT)\",\n      \"explanation\": \"EXTERNAL BEAM RADIATION THERAPY is explicitly listed as a treatment type that may be selected as part of the TREATMENT PLAN (notably in a cervical cancer context), indicating modality-level selection within the plan [Data: Relationships (10251); Entities (4737, 198)].\\n\\nThe dataset also makes the radiation plan concrete through parameters: the TREATMENT PLAN specifies the intended RADIATION DOSE(S) and the NUMBER OF SESSIONS, emphasizing that the plan is not just a choice of therapy but a structured specification of delivery [Data: Relationships (10466, 10467); Entities (4821, 4822, 198)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Patient access, transparency, and recordkeeping are built into the information flow via portals and copies\",\n      \"explanation\": \"ONLINE PORTALS are described as a method for accessing TEST RESULTS over time, and a PORTAL is also described as a place to access copies of test results, establishing a patient-facing retrieval channel for clinical information [Data: Relationships (8076, 14511); Entities (1606, 6339, 154)].\\n\\nIn parallel, patients are instructed to obtain and keep copies of results and reports: TEST RESULTS are linked to GETTING COPIES OF RESULTS AND REPORTS, and guidance includes keeping COPIES OF TEST RESULTS and organizing records (including reports about the specific type of cancer) as part of personal medical documentation [Data: Relationships (2289, 12018, 5465); Entities (1417, 5428, 2930, 1412)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Turnaround time (days to weeks) is a notable operational reality that affects when decisions can be finalized\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The community explicitly notes that it may take DAYS OR WEEKS before all test results are available, and in some cases results might take WEEKS, indicating delayed availability of complete diagnostic evidence [Data: Relationships (8084, 2885); Entities (3875, 1743, 154)].\\n\\nThis timing reality aligns with the portal-and-recordkeeping subnetwork: as results arrive over time, ONLINE PORTALS/PORTAL access and the practice of maintaining copies support longitudinal review and sharing with the care team [Data: Relationships (8076, 14511); Entities (1606, 6339, 2930)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Staging is represented as a pre-treatment construct tied to imaging, with standardized notation\",\n      \"explanation\": \"CLINICAL STAGING (PRE-SURGERY) is defined as the staging determination made before any treatment based on physical exam, biopsy, and imaging tests, and it is described (for throat cancer) as being based on imaging results [Data: Entities (2551); Relationships (4683)].\\n\\nThe community also encodes a notation convention: clinical stage is denoted with a “c” prefix (the CLINICAL STAGE PREFIX (C)), supporting standardized communication of pre-treatment stage [Data: Relationships (4685); Entities (2557, 2551)].\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"rating\": 6.5,\n  \"rating_explanation\": \"Impact is moderately high because the network describes core clinical information flows (test results → diagnosis → risk/staging → treatment planning) that directly influence patient care decisions, while showing no evidence of illicit activity or adversarial intent in the provided data.\"\n}", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null, "reasoning_content": "**Creating a community report**\n\nI need to compile a community report focused on medical test results and treatment plans within the healthcare ecosystem. I'll assess the impact severity, which might be considered low to moderate, but it could affect patient outcomes, so I’d rate it around 6.5. I should include 5 to 10 findings with sections supported by grounded citations. Additionally, I’ll make sure to note missing entities like Diagnosis and Oncologist while using relationships cautiously, only including them if there's clear evidence. The title will be: \"Test Results, Treatment Plans, and Patient Preferences in Cancer Care.\""}, "provider_specific_fields": {"native_finish_reason": "stop"}}], "usage": {"completion_tokens": 2010, "prompt_tokens": 5193, "total_tokens": 7203, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": null, "audio_tokens": null, "reasoning_tokens": 368, "rejected_prediction_tokens": null, "text_tokens": null}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 1536, "text_tokens": null, "image_tokens": null}}, "service_tier": null}}}